1900 Census - Barney, Georgia Status: Published research edition. Household assignments and census totals have been reconciled; uncertain readings remain identified on the relevant household pages. Data Source: 1900 United States Federal Census, Barney Precinct / Militia District 1571, Brooks County, Georgia
Enumeration Total: 992 residents in 201 reconstructed households
Household Pages: Each household below links to a family page based on the reconciled 992-person census dataset. Public Analysis The 1900 census captures Barney just before incorporation, with 992 people in 201 households. Household size ranged from 1 to 13 people, with an average of 4.9 people per household. The record shows a working sawmill, railroad, farm, mercantile, and turpentine community tied together by kinship, boarding arrangements, and labor networks. The relationship counts show why household pages matter: the census includes children, spouses, extended kin, boarders, employees, servants, and neighbors whose later records often explain how Barney families connected across surnames. Age and name readings remain tied to the source notes on the household pages where the encyclopedia flags uncertain handwriting or later-record conflicts. Race Category Count White 611 Black 381 Relationship Groups Category Count Children 521 Heads 201 Spouses 162 Extended or blended kin 77 Boarders, employees, and non-kin 31 Occupation Groups Category Count No occupation listed 718 Agriculture 163 Sawmill and lumber 61 Turpentine and naval stores 13 Commerce and store work 8 Clothing and needlework 7 Railroad and transport 5 Building trades 4 Domestic and service work 4 General labor 4 Medical 2 Manager — unspecified industry 1 Public service 1 School listed as occupation 1 Birthplaces Category Count Georgia 929 North Carolina 32 South Carolina 13 Florida 12 Virginia 3 Alabama 2 Mississippi 1 Early-settler connections Twenty-two residents in the 1900 Barney census currently have 29 graded paths to eight named early settlers of Irwin, Lowndes, or Brooks counties. The published crosswalk explains the evidence and its limits; proposed Strickland and Morgan Swain connections remain under research. Explore early settlers and their Barney descendants. Individual research dossiers The following people named in contemporary reports have dedicated evidence-graded dossiers. Their page